WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.367
Today might

00:00:00.367 --> 00:00:01.001
be the start of a

00:00:01.001 --> 00:00:02.702
pretty volatile week on Wall Street

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:04.170
will get key readings on the U.S.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:04.738
labor market.

00:00:04.738 --> 00:00:06.272
Also testimony, two days of it

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.107
from the Federal Reserve

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:08.341
chairman, Jerome Powell.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.576
The Fed will announce its decision

00:00:09.576 --> 00:00:10.910
on interest rates a little bit later

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:11.378
this month.

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:11.878
This, of course,

00:00:11.878 --> 00:00:12.812
follows eight

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.114
consecutive rate hikes

00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:15.682
going back to March of last year.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.484
Also over the weekend, notably,

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:18.852
Federal Reserve President

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:20.887
Mary Daley of the San Francisco Fed

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:22.222
said in a speech

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:24.290
this weekend, quote, It's clear

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.725
there is more work to do.

00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:26.793
Suggesting that the Fed

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:27.694
will need to raise rates

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:29.195
higher than expected

00:00:29.195 --> 00:00:31.631
and keep them higher for longer.

00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:32.766
But what does it mean for you?

00:00:32.766 --> 00:00:34.534
Former Treasury Secretary Larry Summers

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:35.068
joins us now,

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:36.970
someone who has been spot on

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:38.905
when it comes to the risks and inflation

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:40.440
for a number of

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:40.907
years now,

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:42.208
warning the Biden administration

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:43.443
and others of what is to come.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:44.310
So, Secretary Summers,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.212
thanks very much for your time.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:47.814
It wasn't just Mary Daley of the San

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:48.481
Francisco Fed.

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:50.650
It's also the Minneapolis Federal Reserve

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.985
president, Neel Kashkari,

00:00:51.985 --> 00:00:52.919
who we've had in the program,

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:54.988
Atlanta Fed Chief Raphael Bostic.

00:00:55.021 --> 00:00:55.855
A lot of warnings,

00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:57.524
including the warning from

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.659
Fed Governor Christopher Waller.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:00.994
Do you think the Fed is going

00:01:00.994 --> 00:01:02.462
to need to increase

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.064
interest rates more than expected?

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:05.465
Maybe it's 50 basis

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:08.368
points at the next meeting this month.

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:10.003
Look, I don't think there's

00:01:10.003 --> 00:01:14.007
any question, Poppy, that we do not yet

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:17.010
have inflation on a secure glide path

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:21.347
anywhere near down to the 2% level.

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:23.750
And until the Fed

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.919
can be confident of that,

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:28.755
it's going to have to be tightening

00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.458
rather than easing.

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.327
So my guess is that

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:37.030
rates are going to reach a higher level

00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:41.267
than the market is now expecting

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:43.036
that it's going to certainly reach

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:44.671
a much higher level

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:48.541
than the Fed was expecting last December.

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.677
And that once again,

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:53.980
the data will record that the

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.150
Fed underestimated inflation

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:58.918
and underestimated

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:00.420
how much policy

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:02.655
was going to be necessary.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:03.823
That's a

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:07.393
risky thing because historically,

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:10.430
we don't tend to be able to engineer

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:15.602
soft landings from significant inflation.

00:02:16.035 --> 00:02:20.073
And so my guess is that at some point,

00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:22.809
the Fed will push and push.

00:02:23.109 --> 00:02:25.545
We will not get inflation

00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:28.948
accelerate and skyrocket out of control.

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.417
But my guess is that the process

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:32.185
of bringing down

00:02:32.185 --> 00:02:35.054
inflation will bring on a recession

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:37.290
at some stage

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.826
as it almost always has in the past.

00:02:40.126 --> 00:02:41.728
How much higher?

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:43.796
Larry, I mean, are we talking 6%?

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:46.533
Look what Goldman is saying right now.

00:02:46.533 --> 00:02:49.802
I wouldn't be amazed if we had 6%.

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:51.905
6% would probably not be

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.340
my best guess.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:54.841
My best

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.143
guess would probably be five and a half

00:02:57.143 --> 00:03:01.814
or a little above five and a half.

00:03:02.182 --> 00:03:07.487
But 6% would not surprise me greatly.

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.255
And I think anybody

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.057
who thinks that that's off

00:03:11.057 --> 00:03:11.691
the radar

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:14.961
screen is making a real mistake.

00:03:15.361 --> 00:03:19.566
Given all the uncertainties that we have

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.735
in our economy

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:23.469
right now,

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.572
and I think what people have to do

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:30.276
is just be prepared for a lot of things.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:32.178
The old advice.

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.379
Hope for the best,

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.515
but plan for the worse,

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:38.051
I think, is the right advice for

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:40.119
your listeners.

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:40.386
I think

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:44.057
it's the right advice for companies,

00:03:44.057 --> 00:03:44.357
I think

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:48.094
is the right advice for policymakers

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:50.163
like you.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:51.831
JPMorgan CEO Jamie

00:03:51.831 --> 00:03:53.433
Dimon has also been warning about

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:54.367
what's ahead, especially

00:03:54.367 --> 00:03:56.469
when people deplete all that

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:58.204
COVID stimulus savings.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:03:59.105
Listen to what he said.

00:03:59.105 --> 00:04:01.574
This was on February 23rd on CNBC

00:04:03.876 --> 00:04:04.611
the US economy.

00:04:04.611 --> 00:04:06.412
This is the contradiction here of the US

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.081
economy right now is doing quite well.

00:04:08.081 --> 00:04:09.482
Consumers have a lot of money.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:11.551
They're spending it. Jobs are plentiful.

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:13.253
That's today out in front of us.

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:14.287
There's some scary stuff.

00:04:14.287 --> 00:04:15.188
And you and I know

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:16.189
there's always uncertainty,

00:04:16.189 --> 00:04:18.191
but that's that's a normal thing.

00:04:18.191 --> 00:04:18.458
Okay.

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:19.959
This uncertainty is a little bit

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:21.160
more than that.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.095
Do you agree with him?

00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:23.730
This uncertainty is more than normal

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:24.697
and there is some, quote,

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:25.832
scary stuff ahead.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:27.300
And if you do agree,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:28.334
what does that feel like

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:29.602
for the average American

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.240
Poppy, I've used the term

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:36.542
risk of a widely coyote moment

00:04:36.943 --> 00:04:39.879
to refer to the fact that the economy

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:44.284
could hit an air pocket in a few months.

00:04:45.018 --> 00:04:48.054
My guess is that the overhang

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:48.955
in the savings

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.990
that consumers have accumulated

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.393
has a few more months to run,

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:55.628
but it doesn't have another year to run.

00:04:56.329 --> 00:04:58.665
Businesses seem to be long on

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:02.101
inventories right now,

00:05:02.535 --> 00:05:04.871
businesses are holding on to workers

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:06.472
because there's been a labor shortage

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:08.341
for the last couple of years.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:10.877
But if that starts to go away,

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:13.546
then they're going to feel less pressure

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:17.283
to hold on to workers.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.486
It may be that interest

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:21.220
rates are going

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:23.890
to eventually work their way

00:05:23.890 --> 00:05:24.991
through the system,

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:26.626
and that's going to have

00:05:26.626 --> 00:05:28.094
a significant effect

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:30.630
on employment, for example, in

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:32.665
building houses.

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:35.968
So you've got a variety of dynamics

00:05:36.369 --> 00:05:38.504
that could kick in.

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:39.872
I thought it was interesting

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:42.008
in a survey of households,

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:43.643
they said they were feeling

00:05:43.643 --> 00:05:45.645
really quite good now

00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:47.747
and they were worried about the future.

00:05:48.247 --> 00:05:49.048
And when you ask

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:50.917
some of the big retail companies

00:05:50.917 --> 00:05:52.352
when they reported their earnings

00:05:52.352 --> 00:05:53.553
like Wal-Mart,

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:57.557
they said something quite similar.

00:05:57.857 --> 00:06:02.228
So I think between the risk of inflation

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:05.431
and the risk of

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:09.068
some kind of air pocket,

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:11.371
the Fed is trying to walk along

00:06:11.838 --> 00:06:14.874
a very, very narrow ledge.

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:17.510
And it's not an easy situation.

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:19.345
Know it's a tightrope.

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:21.314
There is no question about that, Larry.

00:06:21.314 --> 00:06:22.115
Summers, thank you.

00:06:22.115 --> 00:06:23.483
And we'll see what

00:06:23.483 --> 00:06:25.818
members of the Senate and the House ask

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:26.753
Fed Chair

00:06:26.753 --> 00:06:29.055
Jay Powell when he testifies this week.

00:06:29.122 --> 00:06:31.324
Appreciate your time this morning.

00:06:31.324 --> 00:06:32.425
Our chief business correspondent,

00:06:32.425 --> 00:06:34.460
Christine Romans here to break it down

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:34.827
there.

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:35.495
They're calling it

00:06:35.495 --> 00:06:37.230
hell week on Wall Street this week

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.431
because there's so much happening

00:06:38.431 --> 00:06:39.499
and it's all really important.

00:06:39.499 --> 00:06:40.800
You've got a Chair Powell

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.568
going to the Hill for a couple of days.

00:06:42.568 --> 00:06:44.237
You got a bunch of different jobs

00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:46.239
statistics until we get to Friday's

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:46.906
jobs report.

00:06:46.906 --> 00:06:47.740
You've got the president's

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:48.641
going to drop his budget

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:50.143
blueprint on Thursday.

00:06:50.143 --> 00:06:51.811
So there's a lot that matters

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:53.980
for your money happening this week.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:55.548
And one of the big issues

00:06:55.548 --> 00:06:55.748
here,

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:57.884
I think, is a very strong job market.

00:06:58.151 --> 00:06:59.051
And that is something

00:06:59.051 --> 00:07:01.287
that has been a problem for the Fed.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.655
We're expecting the job market to be

00:07:02.655 --> 00:07:05.491
maybe 200,000 jobs added

00:07:05.725 --> 00:07:06.959
in the most recent month.

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:08.761
And you can see, remember last month

00:07:08.761 --> 00:07:10.496
how strong that number was.

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:12.165
So the job market continues

00:07:12.165 --> 00:07:13.533
to be very, very strong.

00:07:13.533 --> 00:07:14.801
And we expect we're going to see that

00:07:14.801 --> 00:07:15.568
again this week.

00:07:15.568 --> 00:07:16.636
So why

00:07:16.636 --> 00:07:17.937
why would a strong labor

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:21.040
market be bad news for the Fed? It's

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:21.908
because the Fed

00:07:21.908 --> 00:07:22.275
has been

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:23.276
raising interest rates

00:07:23.276 --> 00:07:24.644
over and over and over again,

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:26.279
and it's not cooling off the job market.

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:27.313
And that strong job market

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:28.047
could continue

00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:30.082
to spin off more inflation

00:07:30.249 --> 00:07:32.251
and the Fed is trying to cool inflation.

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:33.419
So it just shows you

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:35.254
the Fed might have to keep interest rates

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:36.722
higher for longer.

00:07:36.722 --> 00:07:40.092
We know that there are two to jobs open

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:42.195
for every person who is looking for work.

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:44.297
That is a really, really tight

00:07:44.297 --> 00:07:44.931
labor market.

00:07:44.931 --> 00:07:47.400
That chart right there shows you

00:07:48.067 --> 00:07:48.868
that the Fed might

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:50.036
still have a lot of work to do.

00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:51.571
The Fed would like to see things

00:07:51.571 --> 00:07:52.171
cool off a bit.

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:53.506
So we have a strong job market,

00:07:53.506 --> 00:07:54.640
great for Main Street,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:55.975
not so great for Wall Street.

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:58.044
Well, and also really kind of shows you

00:07:58.544 --> 00:07:59.178
the power

00:07:59.178 --> 00:08:00.780
or lack of of the Federal Reserve

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.248
when it comes to these interest rates,

00:08:02.248 --> 00:08:03.216
because if they're not

00:08:03.216 --> 00:08:04.851
impacting the job market,

00:08:04.851 --> 00:08:06.786
are they having any impact?

00:08:06.786 --> 00:08:07.687
And I think that's

00:08:07.687 --> 00:08:08.821
what you're going to see.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:10.890
The Fed chief grilled about this week.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:11.524
You're going to hear him.

00:08:11.524 --> 00:08:13.226
People on the Hill are going to say

00:08:13.226 --> 00:08:14.360
you're going too hard.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:15.661
This is hurting working families

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:16.195
are they're going to say

00:08:16.195 --> 00:08:17.330
you're not going hard enough

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:18.498
getting inflation under control.

00:08:18.498 --> 00:08:19.699
So I think he's going to be criticized

00:08:19.699 --> 00:08:20.399
on both sides.

00:08:20.399 --> 00:08:21.434
But we can see where

00:08:21.434 --> 00:08:22.468
where the Fed interest rates

00:08:22.468 --> 00:08:23.736
have been working in the housing market

00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:24.437
that has slowed,

00:08:24.437 --> 00:08:26.072
but also credit card interest rates.

00:08:26.072 --> 00:08:28.140
And I think this is really the takeaway

00:08:28.374 --> 00:08:30.376
for four kitchen table economics here.

00:08:30.743 --> 00:08:31.310
Credit card

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:32.845
interest rates are at record highs.

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.981
19% for a typical credit card,

00:08:35.314 --> 00:08:37.783
up to 30% for a store card.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:38.651
If you put

00:08:38.651 --> 00:08:40.786
$2,000 on a typical credit card,

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:43.456
you only pay the minimum balance at 19%.

00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:44.690
It would take more than 12 years

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:45.391
to pay it off

00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:46.792
and you would have 30 $200

00:08:46.792 --> 00:08:49.395
in interest on that $2,000 That is just,

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:51.430
that is just more interest

00:08:51.430 --> 00:08:53.266
to initial spend, more interest

00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:54.166
in the initial spend.

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:56.335
And on a store card it's even worse.

00:08:56.335 --> 00:08:58.371
So I think that where you're seeing

00:08:58.771 --> 00:08:59.939
the higher interest rates

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:00.873
really working through

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:02.375
the economy are places like that.

00:09:02.375 --> 00:09:02.642
And that's

00:09:02.642 --> 00:09:03.976
where people have to really be careful.

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.778
I think Caitlin makes the point,

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:07.980
which is the medicine's not working

00:09:08.314 --> 00:09:08.948
enough.

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:11.350
The Fed medicine on interest rates isn't.

00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:12.985
So what does that mean? The Fed have to.

00:09:12.985 --> 00:09:14.020
What else can we do?

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:15.955
Do they raise it to 5%, the target rate?

00:09:15.955 --> 00:09:16.889
Do they raise it, as Goldman

00:09:16.889 --> 00:09:19.191
Sachs suggested, closer to to 6%?

00:09:19.392 --> 00:09:20.660
That means higher rates

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:22.328
for longer in the economy

00:09:22.328 --> 00:09:23.763
and higher rates are to know

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:24.897
if it would work in

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:25.398
higher rates

00:09:25.398 --> 00:09:27.400
is something that that that real people

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:29.402
in working families feel.

00:09:29.402 --> 00:09:31.070
Meanwhile, the job market is strong

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:32.171
right on its surface.

00:09:32.171 --> 00:09:33.706
That should be good news.

00:09:33.706 --> 00:09:34.640
The job market strong.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:35.908
There are a lot of jobs open

00:09:35.908 --> 00:09:36.576
for people out there.

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:37.810
Even with these headlines

00:09:37.810 --> 00:09:40.112
in in tech and in finance of layoffs.

00:09:41.047 --> 00:09:42.548
Overall, the job market is strong

00:09:42.548 --> 00:09:43.816
